Conflict relations:
- duplicate: semantically the same requirement/pitfall;
- near_duplicate: no meaningful independently judgeable distinction;
- sibling_dimension: the candidate semantically MATCHES this existing criterion
  and has therefore drifted from the selected parent into its dimension.

Lexical overlap is not required: a blood-pressure candidate under a heart-rate
parent matches an existing blood-pressure criterion; a hydration candidate under
a folic-acid parent matches an existing hydration criterion. Merely being a
different dimension is NOT sibling drift: if candidate and existing criterion do
not overlap, label it unrelated and do not output it. Logical contradiction with
an opposite-polarity criterion is also not duplication; positive requirements
and penalty conditions may intentionally coexist.

To stay within the output budget, compare every item internally but output rows
ONLY for conflicts. If there are no conflicts, comparisons must be empty. Set
all_compared=true only after checking the entire list.

Return only JSON:
{
  "comparisons": [
    {
      "criterion_id": "exact conflicting id",
      "relation": "duplicate | near_duplicate | sibling_dimension",
      "conflict": true,
      "reason": "brief pairwise reason"
    }
  ],
  "conflict_ids": [],
  "checked_count": 12,
  "all_compared": true,
  "no_conflict": true,
  "valid": true,
  "reason": "brief overall reason"
}

Set valid=true only when all items were compared and no conflict exists.
